FEN = [
("Inland Waterways and Sagarmala", "India possesses an extensive network of rivers, canals, backwaters and creeks, yet only a small fraction of its freight moves by water. The Sagarmala programme and the declaration of over one hundred national waterways aim to correct this imbalance. Water transport consumes considerably less fuel per tonne kilometre than road haulage, generates lower emissions and relieves congestion on overburdened highways. Terminals have been constructed at Varanasi, Sahibganj and Haldia, while dredging is being undertaken to maintain navigable depths during the lean season. Nevertheless, seasonal fluctuations in water levels, inadequate night navigation aids and the absence of reliable last mile connectivity continue to deter shippers. Experts argue that integrating waterways with rail sidings, warehouses and digital cargo tracking, alongside assured minimum depth, is indispensable if inland navigation is to become a dependable component of the national logistics chain."),
("Critical Minerals and the Energy Transition", "The global shift towards electric vehicles, solar panels and battery storage has transformed lithium, cobalt, nickel, graphite and rare earth elements into strategically significant resources. India currently imports most of these critical minerals, which exposes its manufacturing ambitions to supply disruptions and price volatility. To reduce this vulnerability, the government has identified a list of critical minerals, amended mining legislation to permit their auction to private firms and established a joint venture to acquire mineral assets abroad. Exploration has been intensified, and promising deposits have been reported in several states. However, extraction must be balanced against environmental safeguards and the rights of local communities, particularly in forested tribal regions. Equally important is the development of domestic refining capacity and recycling technology, since possessing ore alone does not guarantee a secure and competitive supply chain."),
("Deep Ocean Mission and the Blue Economy", "The oceans surrounding India hold immense economic potential in the form of fisheries, minerals, energy and maritime trade. The Deep Ocean Mission seeks to develop technologies for exploring and sustainably harnessing these resources. A central component is the indigenous manned submersible designed to carry scientists to depths of six thousand metres, enabling direct study of the seabed and its polymetallic nodules. The mission also includes ocean climate advisory services, the mapping of deep sea biodiversity and research into offshore thermal energy. Proponents emphasise that a robust blue economy can generate employment for coastal populations while strengthening national security. Critics, however, caution that seabed mining may irreversibly damage fragile ecosystems about which scientific knowledge remains limited. A precautionary approach, transparent environmental assessment and international cooperation are therefore essential prerequisites."),
("Circular Economy in Construction", "The construction sector is among the largest consumers of raw materials and generators of waste in India. Every year, enormous quantities of sand, gravel, cement and steel are extracted, while demolition debris is frequently dumped on vacant land, riverbanks and roadsides. A circular economy approach seeks to keep materials in productive use for as long as possible. Construction and demolition waste can be processed into recycled aggregates, paver blocks and manufactured sand, thereby reducing pressure on riverbeds that suffer from illegal mining. Several municipal corporations have commissioned processing plants and mandated the use of recycled products in public works. Designing buildings for easy disassembly, adopting durable materials and reusing structural components further extend resource life. Wider adoption, however, requires reliable quality standards, collection systems and procurement policies that do not penalise recycled products in tenders."),
("Biodiversity Targets and Protected Areas", "Biological diversity underpins food security, medicine, water regulation and the livelihoods of millions of people. Recognising the accelerating loss of species and habitats, nations adopted an ambitious global framework that calls for conserving thirty percent of land and sea areas by 2030. India, a megadiverse country with four recognised biodiversity hotspots, has expanded its network of its network of sanctuaries, national parks and reserves managed jointly with communities. Yet protected areas alone cannot halt decline if surrounding landscapes are fragmented by highways, mines and unplanned settlements. Conservation scientists therefore advocate other effective area based measures, such as sacred groves, village forests and well managed wetlands, which are protected by local communities. Strengthening biodiversity management committees, documenting traditional knowledge and ensuring fair sharing of benefits are crucial for converting global commitments into tangible results on the ground."),
("Poshan Abhiyaan and Child Nutrition", "Malnutrition in early childhood has lifelong consequences, affecting physical growth, cognitive development, learning outcomes and future earning capacity. Stunting, wasting and anaemia remain significant public health challenges in India despite steady economic growth. Poshan Abhiyaan was launched to address these problems through a convergent approach involving health, women and child development, sanitation and drinking water departments. Anganwadi workers use smartphones to record the growth of children, track pregnant women and plan home visits. Supplementary nutrition, fortified foods, deworming and counselling on breastfeeding and complementary feeding form the core interventions. Community events during nutrition month aim to change feeding practices within households. Evaluations indicate progress in some indicators, but sustained improvement demands better quality of food, adequate staffing of anganwadi centres and greater attention to maternal health and women's education."),
("Digital Personal Data Protection", "As citizens increasingly share personal information with banks, hospitals, retailers and government platforms, the protection of such data has become a matter of fundamental importance. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act establishes obligations for entities that collect and process personal data, which the law describes as data fiduciaries. They must obtain clear and informed consent, use data only for the stated purpose, implement reasonable security safeguards and report breaches. Individuals are granted rights to access information about the processing of their data, to seek correction and erasure, and to nominate another person to exercise these rights in the event of death or incapacity. Complaints are decided by a specialised board empowered to levy heavy monetary penalties on defaulting entities. Critics have raised concerns regarding broad exemptions for government agencies, while industry seeks clarity on compliance timelines and cross border data transfers."),
("Opening of the Space Sector", "For decades, space activities in India were conducted almost exclusively by the national space agency. Recent reforms have opened the sector to private enterprises, permitting them to build satellites, develop launch vehicles and provide space based services. A dedicated authorisation body evaluates proposals from private firms and allows them to use government testing facilities and launch infrastructure. Several start ups have already conducted sub orbital launches and placed small satellites in orbit, while others are developing earth observation constellations and satellite internet solutions. The government has also permitted foreign direct investment in segments of the space industry. Advocates believe that private participation will expand India's share of the global space economy, generate highly skilled employment and free the national agency to concentrate on scientific missions. A comprehensive space activities law, however, is still awaited."),
("MSME Credit and Receivables Financing", "Micro, small and medium enterprises contribute substantially to manufacturing output, exports and employment, yet many of them struggle with chronic shortages of working capital. A major reason is the delay in payments from large corporate buyers and government departments, which can stretch for several months. To address this problem, an electronic trade receivables discounting system was created, allowing small suppliers to upload invoices accepted by buyers and have them financed by banks through competitive bidding. The supplier receives payment quickly, while the buyer settles the amount with the financier on the due date. Additional measures include a legal requirement that buyers disclose and settle dues to small enterprises within prescribed periods, along with credit guarantee schemes. Despite these initiatives, onboarding of buyers remains limited, and many tiny enterprises lack the documentation required to benefit from formal credit."),
("The Indian Carbon Market", "Carbon markets put a price on greenhouse gas emissions and thereby encourage industries to reduce them in the most cost effective manner. India has notified a framework for a domestic carbon credit trading scheme that will operate in two modes. Under the compliance mechanism, energy intensive sectors such as cement, aluminium, steel and refineries will be assigned emission intensity targets. Units that perform better than their targets can earn tradable certificates, while those that fall short have to buy them. Under the voluntary offset mechanism, projects that reduce or remove emissions, such as afforestation or renewable energy, can generate credits for sale. Credible monitoring, reporting and verification are critical to prevent double counting and inflated claims. If designed robustly, the market can mobilise private investment for decarbonisation while helping the country move towards its long term net zero commitment."),
("Urban Flooding and Drainage", "Heavy downpours now regularly bring large Indian cities to a standstill. Roads turn into rivers, underpasses are submerged, trains are suspended and low lying settlements are inundated for days. Although extreme rainfall events are becoming more frequent, much of the damage is the result of human decisions. Lakes, ponds and natural drainage channels have been encroached upon or filled for construction, while concrete surfaces prevent rainwater from soaking into the ground. Storm water drains are frequently clogged with plastic and debris, and in many neighbourhoods they were never designed for current rainfall intensities. Urban planners recommend restoring water bodies, mapping flood prone zones, enforcing restrictions on construction in floodplains and adopting sponge city techniques such as permeable pavements, bioswales and rooftop harvesting. Real time flood forecasting and coordinated action among municipal agencies are equally essential."),
("Ageing Population and Elder Care", "India is often described as a young nation, but the proportion of elderly citizens is rising steadily because of increasing life expectancy and declining fertility. By the middle of this century, nearly one in five Indians is projected to be above sixty years of age. This demographic transition carries profound implications for health care, social security and family structures. Many older persons suffer from multiple chronic illnesses, limited mobility and cognitive decline, yet geriatric services remain scarce outside major cities. A large share of the elderly, especially women, lack pensions or savings and depend entirely on their children. Policy responses include health insurance coverage for senior citizens, the maintenance law that obliges children to support their parents and day care centres run by voluntary organisations. Experts stress the need for trained caregivers, age friendly public spaces and opportunities for productive engagement."),
("Strengthening Coastal Security", "India's long coastline, numerous islands and extensive exclusive economic zone present formidable security challenges. The terrorist attack on Mumbai in 2008, in which assailants arrived by sea, exposed serious gaps in coastal surveillance. Since then, a multi layered security architecture has been established. The Navy is responsible for overall maritime security, the Coast Guard patrols territorial waters, and state marine police guard the shallow waters close to the shore. A chain of coastal radar stations, an automatic identification system for vessels and a national command, control and intelligence network now provide a common operational picture. Fishing boats have been registered and fitted with transponders, and fishermen are encouraged to act as the eyes and ears of security agencies. Regular exercises test coordination among agencies, although shortages of manpower and boats in marine police stations persist."),
("Geographical Indications and Exports", "A geographical indication identifies a product as originating from a particular region whose climate, soil or traditional skill gives it distinctive qualities. Darjeeling tea, Basmati rice, Kanchipuram silk, Alphonso mangoes and Banarasi brocade are well known examples registered in India. Such recognition protects producers against imitation and allows them to command premium prices in domestic and international markets. Hundreds of products have now been registered, ranging from handicrafts and textiles to agricultural produce and food items. However, registration by itself does not guarantee economic benefit. Many artisans and farmers are unaware that their product has received this status, authorised user registration remains low and enforcement against counterfeit goods is weak. Building brands, ensuring quality control through producer organisations and connecting registered products with export promotion councils and online marketplaces are necessary steps to translate legal protection into higher incomes."),
("Kavach and Railway Safety", "Collisions between trains, often caused by signals passed at danger or human error, have been among the most tragic accidents on Indian Railways. Kavach is an indigenously developed automatic train protection system designed to prevent such accidents. It continuously exchanges information between locomotives, stations and trackside equipment through radio communication. If a loco pilot fails to apply the brakes when approaching a red signal or exceeds the permitted speed, the system automatically applies them. It can also stop two trains heading towards each other on the same line. The technology has received the highest safety integrity certification and is being deployed on high density routes in phases. Installation is complex because it requires towers, optical fibre, track mounted tags and equipment in thousands of locomotives. Experts emphasise that technology must be complemented by adequate rest for crews and timely maintenance of assets."),
("Language Technology and Bhashini", "India's linguistic diversity is a cultural treasure, but it also creates barriers in accessing digital services, most of which are designed primarily in English. The national language translation mission, known as Bhashini, aims to break these barriers by developing artificial intelligence tools for speech recognition, machine translation and text to speech in Indian languages. Large datasets of speech and text are being collected through crowdsourcing, in which citizens contribute recordings and validate translations. The resulting models are made available to government departments, start ups and researchers through open interfaces. Applications include real time translation of official websites, voice based access to welfare schemes for people who cannot read, and multilingual communication at public events. Challenges remain in covering low resource languages and dialects, ensuring accuracy in specialised domains and protecting the privacy of contributors."),
("Rooftop Solar for Households", "Rooftop solar systems allow households to generate electricity from sunlight falling on their own roofs. A typical home installation of two or three kilowatts can meet a large share of monthly consumption, and surplus power can be exported to the grid through net metering, which reduces the electricity bill further. A national scheme now offers substantial central financial assistance to households that install such systems, together with collateral free loans at concessional interest rates. Applications, vendor selection and subsidy release are handled through an online portal to minimise paperwork. Apart from savings for consumers, decentralised generation reduces transmission losses and eases peak demand on distribution companies. Challenges include the reluctance of some distribution companies to process net metering promptly, inadequate after sales service in small towns and the limited roof space available in crowded urban housing. Community solar models and standardised quality certification for vendors could help expand adoption considerably."),
("Public Procurement and GeM", "Government departments purchase goods and services worth enormous sums every year, ranging from office stationery and computers to vehicles and consultancy. Traditional procurement through paper tenders was often slow, opaque and vulnerable to cartelisation. The Government e Marketplace was created as an online platform where sellers can register, list their products and participate in bids, while buyers can compare prices and place orders transparently. Purchases by central ministries and public sector undertakings through the portal have risen sharply, and many states have also adopted it. The platform has opened government markets to small enterprises, women entrepreneurs and start ups that previously found it difficult to compete. Nevertheless, sellers have reported delays in payments and concerns about quality of goods supplied by some vendors. Continuous monitoring, user feedback and strict penalties for misconduct are necessary to maintain trust."),
("Telemedicine and eSanjeevani", "Shortages of doctors and specialists in rural and remote areas have long prevented millions of Indians from receiving timely medical advice. Telemedicine offers a practical way to bridge this gap. The national telemedicine service, eSanjeevani, enables patients to consult doctors through video calls either directly from their phones or with the assistance of a health worker at a village health and wellness centre. Specialists at district hospitals and medical colleges can examine cases referred by primary care providers and recommend treatment. The service expanded dramatically during the pandemic when physical visits were restricted, and it has since recorded hundreds of millions of consultations. Telemedicine reduces travel costs and lost wages for patients, but it cannot replace physical examination in every situation. Reliable connectivity, integration with electronic health records and clear guidelines on prescribing medicines remain important for its sustained success."),
("Dam Safety in India", "India ranks among the countries with the largest number of large dams, which provide water for irrigation, drinking supply and hydroelectric power and help control floods. A significant proportion of these structures, however, are more than half a century old. Ageing concrete, sediment accumulation in reservoirs, inadequate spillway capacity and changing rainfall patterns increase the risk of failure, which could have catastrophic consequences for communities downstream. The Dam Safety Act provides for a national committee to formulate policies, a national authority to implement them and state level organisations responsible for surveillance, inspection and maintenance. Owners of dams must prepare emergency action plans, conduct periodic risk assessments and install instruments that monitor structural behaviour. A rehabilitation programme supported by international financing is upgrading hundreds of dams. Transparent sharing of inspection reports with the public would further strengthen accountability."),
]
